FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/runtime.rs:551 — `time.sleep` passes an unchecked Redblue `f64` to `Duration::from_secs_f64`, which panics on negative, NaN, infinite, or overflowing input (e.g. `say time.sleep(-1)`), unwinding the interpreter thread into uncatchable `The interpreter thread stopped unexpectedly` — the exact panic class this phase claimed to remove — validate `seconds.is_finite() && seconds >= 0.0` with a bounded range and return `Error::Runtime` otherwise.
2. [MAJOR] tests/time_builtin_test.rs:70 — `edge_time_format_of_a_pre_epoch_timestamp_names_that_date` discards the program value (`match eval(...) { Ok(_) => {} }`); `say` returns `Nothing`, so the test passes whether the output is `1969-12-31` or the buggy `1970-01-01` and cannot pin the central bug fix — assert on the returned `Value` or on `expect` instead of `Ok(_)`.
3. [MAJOR] tests/time_builtin_test.rs:162 — `time_now_seconds_is_a_positive_number` evaluates `say now.seconds > 0` and discards the result, so a zero/negative `seconds` field still yields `Ok(Nothing)` and the test passes — bind the comparison and assert the `Value` is `YesNo(true)`.
4. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:591 — `time.unix` has no range check on the `chrono` parse result (`parsed.and_utc().timestamp()`); REPORT.md:206 admits the failure mode was not examined, so a far-future input like `time.unix("9999999999-01-01 00:00:00")` may panic inside `chrono` past every `Result` — settle with a probe of that input and bound/refuse out-of-range dates as `Runtime` error.
